module honest-alerts-pure

  layer application
  note "the boundaryless public mailbox, lifecycle and schema-manifest library in the honest-alerts distribution (spec 4.1, 7 and 10b). These four exports and the three input doors in honest-alerts make up the literal seven-name honest_alerts.__all__ in any order. Other existing bindings remain available by explicit named import (spec 1.3)."

  inputs = { }
  outputs = { }

  type ActorRef = foreign "honest-alerts.ActorRef"
  type SessionId = foreign "honest-alerts.SessionId"
  type Message = foreign "honest-alerts.Message"

  type Fault = { is_err:   bool
                 code:     str
                 message:  str
                 category: FaultCategory
                 results:  list<any>
                 detail:   dict<str, any> } note "the failure an honest-alerts function returns as a bare union arm (value | Fault), honest-type's canonical fault built field for field (honest-type section 13.2, honest-errors section 2, 3). is_err is the reserved boolean tag, always true, the mark is_fault reads to tell the fault arm from the value arm; it is reserved framework-wide, so no non-fault alerts value carries a field of that name. code is the specific reason, one of the section 6b codes (for example unregistered_message_type on a send of an unregistered message_type, or append_failed when the alert.sent append fails). message is the human sentence naming what went wrong and what to do instead. category is who is responsible, a bounded value, never free text: one of FaultCategory, client or server (honest-type section 13.2); a fault with no category is itself a server error, so category is always one of those two declared values. results is [] except where a function accumulates many results. detail carries the enumerable facts a test or caller reads without parsing the message. Two fields minimum, the code to branch on and the message to read, with is_err the arm tag. The bare union makes the undifferentiated ok/err envelope impossible to express."

  set fault_categories = { "client" : "the caller's input or request is wrong; changing it is what fixes the failure", "server" : "honest-alerts' own logic or a dependency is broken; the caller cannot fix it by changing input" } note "who is responsible for a fault, the two-value closed set honest-type defines in its Fault schema (section 13.2); a fault with no category is itself a server error, so category, where present, is always one of these two"
  vocabulary FaultCategory = { fault_categories } note "who is responsible for this fault, client or server"

  dispatch scope_terminated = { "session" -> terminated_session, "actor" -> terminated_actor, "broadcast" -> terminated_broadcast } note "the acknowledged-condition termination, dispatched on the message's ack_scope (section 4.1), one handler per member of the ack_scopes set and a no-match row. is_terminated selects a handler by the message's ack_scope and the handler answers, from the alert.acknowledged event slice, whether this message has been acknowledged for the viewer the read is taken for: terminated_session when an ack's acking session equals viewing_session; terminated_actor when an ack is by the recipient actor; terminated_broadcast when an ack is by any recipient. There is no if ladder; the selection is this table. The no-match row is not a key here because ack_scope is a bounded AckScope fixed at message-build time, so a value the table does not carry is a program bug, not client input: is_terminated builds a server Fault via alerts_fault (code unknown_ack_scope) for the no-match case rather than returning a silent False, and that Fault is the table's no-match row expressed in the language the grammar has"

  set alert_states = { "created" : "the message has been built and queued, the initial state", "delivered" : "a channel delivered the message", "read" : "the DOM actor rendered the message, an impression", "acknowledged" : "the message was acknowledged, its ack_scope met, terminal", "actioned" : "the recipient gave a non-acknowledge response, terminal", "escalated" : "no ack arrived within the escalation window and the message was forwarded", "expired" : "the TTL was reached with no acknowledgment, terminal", "failed" : "delivery failed across all channels, terminal" } note "the eight states a message instance may be in (section 7); created is initial, and acknowledged, actioned, expired and failed are terminal. A state is reached only through a declared transition in advance()"
  vocabulary AlertState = { alert_states } note "the current lifecycle state of a message instance"

  set alert_events = { "deliver" : "a channel delivered the message", "read" : "the DOM actor rendered it", "acknowledge" : "the recipient acknowledged it", "action" : "the recipient gave a non-acknowledge response", "escalate" : "the escalation window elapsed with no ack", "expire" : "the TTL elapsed with no ack", "fail" : "delivery failed across all channels" } note "the seven events that drive a message through its lifecycle (section 7); each, applied to a state, either names a declared transition to a next state or is undeclared from that state. Each state transition produces a matching alert.* event in honest-observe"
  vocabulary AlertEvent = { alert_events } note "the lifecycle event being applied to a message instance"

  fn alert_tables_schema : () -> any
    note "returns the three alerts-owned honest-persist table manifests as data: alert_routes (the routing table, section 5), alert_deliveries (the delivery queue, section 6.2), and alert_registered_types (the message-type registry, section 3b), all living in the same honest-persist database as the honest-observe event log so run_send can write the delivery rows and append alert.sent across both inside one transaction (section 6.1, 10b). It is the honest-alerts twin of honest-observe's event_log_schema (honest-observe section 10). Each manifest names that table's columns and their types and the indexes the module's reads need, exactly as section 10b declares them: alert_routes keyed by route_id with message_type (supporting a trailing wildcard), a nullable sender_type, channels and a nullable escalation as JSON text (a list of ChannelConfig and an EscalationRule), an integer priority, and an index on message_type; alert_deliveries keyed by delivery_id with message_id, route_id, channel, recipient as JSON text (an ActorRef), an integer deliver_at in epoch seconds UTC, a status of pending, delivered or failed, and indexes on (status, deliver_at) and on message_id; alert_registered_types keyed by message_type with message_versions as JSON text (the closed set of version strings). Unlike the event log these tables are not append-only: alert_deliveries rows move from pending to delivered or failed (section 6.2) and alert_registered_types accepts a byte-identical re-registration as a no-op (section 3b.3); the manifest declares the mutable table shape honest-persist enforces. Pure: it returns the manifest as data, reads no world and takes no argument, because the schema is fixed. The return is any because honest-persist owns the manifest type, exactly as honest-observe's event_log_schema returns its manifest as any. It is reached by import so honest-persist's migration can create the three tables from one declared shape, and the module's table-touching functions (write_delivery and the delivery reader against alert_deliveries, read_routes against alert_routes, read_registered_type and write_registered_type against alert_registered_types) read their table name and columns from this manifest rather than hardcoding them."

  fn alerts_fault : (code: str, message: str, category: FaultCategory, detail: dict<str, any>) -> Fault
    note "builds one honest-alerts Fault record from its parts, the single place honest-alerts assembles a fault (honest-errors section 2, 3; honest-type section 13.2). code is the specific reason, drawn from the section 6b code space: the client codes unregistered_message_type, message_version_not_registered, missing_message_version, invalid_termination, invalid_registration and refused_registration. Server codes include registration_write_failed, append_failed, ack_append_failed, emit_seam_failed, unknown_ack_scope, unknown_termination_condition and invalid_transition; message is the human sentence naming what went wrong and what to do instead; category is who is responsible, a FaultCategory member, client or server, never free text; detail is the structured context a test reads without parsing the message. It sets the reserved is_err tag true and returns the Fault, carried by the return as a bare union arm and recognized by is_fault, never raised and never wrapped in an ok/err envelope. Pure: it reads no world. run_send, run_register_message_type, run_acknowledge, build_message, build_registered_type, supervise and advance invoke it."

  fn advance : (state: AlertState, event: AlertEvent) -> AlertState | Fault invokes alerts_fault
    note "the message lifecycle state machine as a pure transition function (section 7). Given the current AlertState and an AlertEvent, it returns the next AlertState when the pair names a declared transition, and a Fault via alerts_fault with code invalid_transition (category server, section 6b) when it does not (a (state, event) pair the lifecycle transition table does not declare, that is, an event undeclared from that state). The declared transitions are exactly those in section 7: created deliver->delivered, created fail->failed, created expire->expired; delivered read->read, delivered acknowledge->acknowledged, delivered action->actioned, delivered escalate->escalated, delivered expire->expired; read acknowledge->acknowledged, read action->actioned, read escalate->escalated, read expire->expired; escalated acknowledge->acknowledged, escalated action->actioned, escalated expire->expired. acknowledged, actioned, expired and failed are terminal: any event from them is undeclared and faults. Pure: a state and an event in, the next state or a fault out, no world; the lifecycle stays declared data and a pure function, never an exposed object. Reached by import. An invalid_transition Fault carries the supplied state and event in detail.state and detail.event."

  fn mailbox : (actor_ref: ActorRef, at_time: int, events: list<any>, viewing_session: SessionId | NOTHING) -> list<Message> | Fault invokes is_terminated
    note "an actor's mailbox as a pure projection over the events handed to it: which messages addressed to this actor have not yet terminated at at_time, for the viewer the read is taken for (section 4.1). A mailbox is not a data structure and honest-alerts never queries honest-observe's raw log; per honest-observe section 6 a projection is the only read path and there is no raw query interface. The caller selects the event set through an honest-observe projection and passes it as events, a list of honest-observe Event records (any because honest-observe owns that type); mailbox folds over exactly those and reads nothing else. viewing_session is the DOM session this read is taken for, an explicit SessionId or NOTHING, never a defaulted optional: a real DOM read passes the session the page is rendered for; a viewer that is not a DOM session belongs to no DOM session and passes NOTHING (the non-DOM viewer view). mailbox passes viewing_session straight through to is_terminated unchanged and reads no session off actor_ref (ActorRef carries no session, section 4.1). It keeps the alert.sent events whose recipient matches actor_ref and whose sent_at is at or before at_time, invokes is_terminated for each (handing it actor_ref, at_time, events and viewing_session) to drop the terminated ones, and returns the pending Messages ordered by sent_at ascending. is_terminated can return a server Fault on the no-match row of its ack_scope dispatch (an ack_scope value the table does not carry); that Fault travels by return, so mailbox returns list<Message> | Fault and surfaces it rather than swallowing it. Pure: a fold over the given events, no world. It is the read-back surface the DOM actor and any caller reach by import, never a door (section 4.2)."

  fn terminated_session : (acks: list<any>, actor_ref: ActorRef, viewing_session: SessionId | NOTHING) -> bool
    note "the session-scoped row of the scope_terminated dispatch (section 4.1): answers whether a session-scoped message has been acknowledged for the viewer the read is taken for. acks is the alert.acknowledged event slice for this message (every event whose payload.message_id equals the message's id), already selected by is_terminated. actor_ref is the actor whose mailbox is being computed, unused by this row and present only so every row of the dispatch shares one signature. viewing_session is the DOM session the read is taken for. It is terminated only when viewing_session is not NOTHING AND some ack's acking session (payload.session on the alert.acknowledged event) equals viewing_session. viewing_session of NOTHING is the non-DOM viewer view and equals no session, so a session dismissal never ends a non-DOM viewer's read: the NOTHING case returns false here, never matching. A session-scoped ack by one DOM session therefore terminates the message only for that same session; other sessions of the same actor still see it. Pure: an event slice and the viewer in, a boolean out, no world and no fault."

  fn terminated_actor : (acks: list<any>, actor_ref: ActorRef, viewing_session: SessionId | NOTHING) -> bool
    note "the actor-scoped row of the scope_terminated dispatch (section 4.1): answers whether an actor-scoped message has been acknowledged for every viewer. acks is the alert.acknowledged event slice for this message, already selected by is_terminated. actor_ref is the recipient actor whose mailbox is being computed, pure identity read for its id. viewing_session is present only so every row shares one signature and is ignored here: an actor-scoped ack terminates for every viewer, the waiter (viewing_session = NOTHING) included. It is terminated when some ack's payload.actor.id equals actor_ref.id (payload.actor is an ActorRef, so its id is read as payload.actor.id, never as a payload.actor_id field), that is, when the recipient actor acknowledged on any one of its sessions. Pure: an event slice and the actor in, a boolean out, no world and no fault."

  fn terminated_broadcast : (acks: list<any>, actor_ref: ActorRef, viewing_session: SessionId | NOTHING) -> bool
    note "the broadcast-scoped row of the scope_terminated dispatch (section 4.1): answers whether a broadcast-scoped message has been acknowledged for every viewer. acks is the alert.acknowledged event slice for this message, already selected by is_terminated. actor_ref and viewing_session are present only so every row shares one signature and are both ignored here: a broadcast-scoped ack by any one recipient terminates for every viewer, the waiter (viewing_session = NOTHING) included. It is terminated when the acks slice is non-empty, that is, when any recipient acknowledged. Pure: an event slice in, a boolean out, no world and no fault."

  fn event_at_or_after : (timestamp: any, sent_at: int) -> bool note "compares a candidate event timestamp to the Message sent_at UTC epoch seconds in the event-termination branch (Alerts spec 3.1 and 4.1, Observe spec 2.1). Legacy integer timestamps are UTC epoch seconds. Canonical Observe strings are UTC ISO 8601 with six fractional digits and a Z suffix. Decode the full instant and compare at integer microsecond precision against sent_at multiplied by one million. Equality matches. An absent, malformed or unsupported timestamp, including a boolean, gives false for that candidate; it is not treated as the epoch and does not halt scanning later candidates. Reads no clock and changes neither event order nor the type or payload filters."

  fn uuid7_from_parts : (unix_ms: int, random_a: int, random_b: int) -> str note "assembles the UUID v7 message identifier from caller-supplied data (Alerts spec 3.1, RFC 9562 sections 5.7 and 6.1). unix_ms is the nonnegative UTC Unix epoch milliseconds from the reader. random_a and random_b are unsigned big-endian integers from its two-byte and eight-byte entropy samples, respectively. In most-significant-bit order, concatenate the low 48 timestamp bits, version 0111, the low 12 bits of random_a, variant 10 and the low 62 bits of random_b. Return lowercase canonical hyphenated UUID text. No clock, entropy, counter or other state is read here."

  fn is_terminated : (msg: Message, actor_ref: ActorRef, at_time: int, events: list<any>, viewing_session: SessionId | NOTHING) -> bool | Fault invokes scope_terminated, alerts_fault, event_at_or_after
    note "decides whether one message has terminated for the viewer the read is taken for at at_time, the predicate mailbox folds with (section 4.1). msg is the message under test; actor_ref is the actor whose mailbox is being computed, pure identity read for its id when the termination is actor-scoped (it carries no session); at_time is the moment the question is asked; events is the same honest-observe event slice mailbox was handed (any because honest-observe owns the Event type); viewing_session is the DOM session this read is taken for, an explicit SessionId or NOTHING (the waiter view), never a defaulted optional, passed through from mailbox unchanged. It reads msg.termination and branches on its condition exactly as section 4.1 specifies. For ttl, terminated when at_time is past sent_at plus ttl_seconds. For event, terminated when an event of the spec's event_type at or after sent_at matches the optional event_filter. For never, never terminated. For acknowledged, it selects the alert.acknowledged event slice for this message (every event whose payload.message_id equals the message's id) and dispatches on the message's ack_scope through the scope_terminated table, one row per AckScope member plus a no-match row, never an if ladder: the session row terminates only when an ack's acking session (payload.session on the alert.acknowledged event, not a payload.session_id field) equals viewing_session (and viewing_session of NOTHING never matches, so a session dismissal never ends a non-DOM viewer's read); the actor row terminates for every viewer on an ack by the recipient actor (payload.actor.id equals actor_ref.id, read as payload.actor.id because payload.actor is an ActorRef, never as a payload.actor_id field); the broadcast row terminates for every viewer on an ack by any recipient. The no-match row is reached only if ack_scope is a value the table does not carry; because ack_scope is a bounded AckScope fixed at message-build time that is a program bug, not client input, so is_terminated returns a server Fault built via alerts_fault (code unknown_ack_scope, category server) rather than a silent False, and that Fault is the table's no-match row. It consults no lifecycle state machine: termination is decided purely from the event slice, the termination spec and the viewer, so this is a self-contained predicate. Pure: a message, an event slice and the viewer in, a boolean or a Fault out, no world. The Fault travels by return: mailbox returns list<Message> | Fault because this predicate can. Reached by import as part of the mailbox surface. An unknown_ack_scope or unknown_termination_condition Fault carries the corresponding declared alternatives in detail.accepted and the unmatched value in detail.arrived."

  entry "export honest_alerts" -> mailbox
  entry "export honest_alerts" -> is_terminated
  entry "export honest_alerts" -> advance
  entry "export honest_alerts" -> alert_tables_schema
